Here is an implementation of the `sort` method using the insertion sort algorithm:

```java
public void sort(Comparator<T> order) {
    Queue<T> tempQueue = new LinkedList<>();

    while (!this.isEmpty()) {
        T currentEntry = this.dequeue();
        insertInOrder(currentEntry, tempQueue, order);
    }

    // Transfer the sorted elements back to this queue
    while (!tempQueue.isEmpty()) {
        this.enqueue(tempQueue.dequeue());
    }
}

private void insertInOrder(T currentEntry, Queue<T> queue, Comparator<T> order) {
    if (queue.isEmpty() || order.compare(currentEntry, queue.peek()) <= 0) {
        // Insert at the front of the queue
        queue.enqueue(currentEntry);
    } else {
        // Find the correct position to insert the currentEntry
        T frontEntry;
        do {
            frontEntry = queue.dequeue();
            queue.enqueue(frontEntry);
        } while (!queue.isEmpty() && order.compare(currentEntry, queue.peek()) > 0);

        // Insert the currentEntry at the correct position
        queue.enqueue(currentEntry);
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a temporary queue to store the sorted elements while dequeuing from the original queue. The `insertInOrder` method places the current entry in the correct position within the temporary queue by comparing it with each element already in the queue. The sorted elements are then transferred back to the original queue.

